Activity 1, Link 1 (TutorialsPoint: Web Browser)
Describe: This article helps define a web browser. A web browser is "an application software that allows us to view and explore information 
on the web. A web browser can show "text, audio, video, animation and more." There are seemingly no limits when it comes to informing a viewer 
and "It is the responsibility of a web browser to interpret text and commands contained on the web page," meaning that a web browser's job is to search 
for associated key terms of stored information to quite literally inform whoever or whatever. Examples of common web browsers are Internet Explorer, Google Chrome, 
Firefox, Opera and Safari. Each web browser has subtle differences but mostly overlap with three basic components: Controller/Dispatcher, Interpreter and Client Programs. 
A controller works as a control unit in the CPU. It takes input from the keyboard or mouse, interprets it and makes other services work on the basis of the input it receives." 
An Interpreter "receives the information from the controller and executes the instruction line by line" and a "Client Program describes the specific protocol that will be used to access a particular service".
Learned: I didn't realize how many web browsers were out there. I thought there was only Google.
Envision: Finding different information on different web browsers.

Activity 1, Link 2 (LearnFree: Internet Tips)
Describe: LearnFree offers "tips and strategies" about navigating the internet through 15 "episodes" which appear to be articles. 
The first episode is called "Getting started with the Internet." To get started, you need to purchase an internet connection via 
an internet provider to get to the internet. Other means of establishing an internet connection include Wi-Fi or cellular internet. 
Information is found on websites, which are accessed through a web browser. "A website is a collection of text, images, or other resources. 
Websites can resemble other forms of media such as newspaper articles. The purpose of a website can be almost anything: a news platform, 
an advertisement, an online library, a forum for sharing images, etc. A web browser allows you to connect to and view websites. 
The web browser itself is not the Internet, but it displays pages on the Internet. Each website has a unique address. 
By typing this address into your web browser, you can connect to that website and your web browser will display it." 
Other notable concepts mentioned include Hyperlinks, which are other website links found on a website. 
They are often highlighted in a different way to the rest of the page's fonts for differentiation. 
If these links are clicked, they will load onto a different page and Search Engines,"a specialized website that's designed 
to help you find other websites. If you type keywords or a phrase into a search engine, it will display a list of websites relevant to your search terms."
Learned: I didn't know that a link within another website was called a Hyperlink, and now I know.
Envision: Being aware of Hyperlinks can make you more inclined to find original sources.

Activity 3, Links 1-3 (LearnFree: Chrome, LearnFree: Edge, LearnFree: Firefox)
Describe: "A web browser is a type of software that allows you to find and view websites on the Internet. 
For many years, especially in the early 2000s, there was one browser that almost everyone used: Microsoft's Internet Explorer. 
In the past, Internet Explorer was pre-installed on any PC you buy. Starting in the mid-2000s, other software companies 
introduced new browsers, including Mozilla Firefox and Google Chrome. At the time, these browsers allowed for more experimental 
features. Because of this, web developers began creating websites with browsers like Chrome and Firefox in mind, which meant 
they didn't always work as well in Internet Explorer. It's always a good idea to keep at least two web browsers on your computer 
or mobile device. This way, if a website isn't working correctly, you can try opening the page in a different web browser. 
More often than not, this trick can solve a lot of problems you might encounter online."
Learned: I didn't know that Internet Explorer was the first search engine. Interesting.
Envision: Using multiple browsers when encountering errors can be useful. 
